{
  "summary": "One finding on this computer needs your attention soon. A startup shortcut named like OneDrive actually runs a hidden, encoded PowerShell command every time you sign in, which is a pattern malware often uses. Look at that one first and follow the fix shown in the report. The other finding is a low-level reminder to confirm you recognize an administrator account.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Hidden PowerShell command in your Startup folder disguised as OneDrive",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-SUSPICIOUS:3b484bd3"
      ],
      "explanation": "A shortcut called OneDrive.lnk sits in your personal Startup folder, so it runs every time you sign in. It doesn't open OneDrive. It launches PowerShell, a built-in Windows tool that can run commands, with options that hide its window and scramble (encode) the command so you can't easily read it. Normal programs rarely start this way, and giving the shortcut a familiar name like OneDrive is a common way to avoid being noticed. The scan can't say for certain that this is malware, but it deserves a close look soon. Check the fix shown in the report for this finding. If you didn't set this up yourself, consider getting help from someone you trust with computers."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Confirm you recognize this administrator account",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "The computer has a local account (<computer>\\<account-2>) with administrator rights, meaning it can change anything on the computer. Having a separate admin account is often good practice, so this may be completely normal. Just make sure it's an account you or someone in your household created. An admin account you don't recognize can be a sign of a break-in. That matters more here because of the suspicious startup entry above. The report shows what to do if you don't recognize it."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
